# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the sanitizer lane (run_sanitizers.sh wires
# this in via LSAN_OPTIONS; Linux only — macOS ASan has no leak detector).
#
# Scope discipline: every rule below names a dependency LIBRARY MODULE, and a
# rule matches only when a frame of the ALLOCATION stack lands in that module.
# frehg's own allocations (operator new, Kokkos views, std containers) never
# allocate through these modules, so they remain fully leak-checked. The lane's
# ASan (overflows, use-after-free) and UBSan coverage is unaffected by this
# file entirely — it filters the end-of-process leak report only.
#
# libhwloc / libmpich: a process that calls MPI_Init WITHOUT a hydra proxy
# (singleton mode — the unit gtest binary, frehg --validate, the r1
# run-record round-trip revalidation) makes MPICH load the hardware topology
# itself (hwloc_topology_load) and park it in process-global state that
# MPI_Finalize does not free: a constant ~22 KB / 269 allocations per
# process. Hydra-launched ranks receive the topology from the proxy and
# report no leak — which is why the mpi.* tests were green while every
# singleton invocation exited nonzero on the leak report (the r1 gate and
# the --validate suite judge by exit code, so they failed with "VALID" on
# their own stdout).
leak:libhwloc
leak:libmpich
#
# libpetsc: registry / function-list state (kh_resize_HMapFunc,
# PetscFunctionListCreate_Private) that outlives PetscFinalize, plus objects
# abandoned by the unit tests that deliberately drive fatal paths
# mid-construction (LinearSystem.UnknownPreconditionerIsFatal,
# ApiMisuseIsFatal, ...): log::fatal throws, the half-built KSP/PC is
# abandoned by design — in production that path is followed by process
# death.
#   Accepted blind spot: a genuinely leaked frehg-HELD PETSc object (an
# undestroyed Mat/KSP) also allocates through libpetsc and is suppressed by
# this rule. That coverage was never real — the leak checker had never run
# before this file existed (dev machines are macOS) — and frehg-side
# allocations, where a leak would live in code this project can fix, stay
# covered.
leak:libpetsc
